WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.009 --> 00:00:07.870
Doug Jeffrey: Educated at the University of
Rochester and at the Wilson School of Public

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:11.150
and International Affairs at Princeton.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:14.650
He began his career at the Federal Reserve
Bank of New York.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:22.060
He served as chief economist and senior managing
director at Bear Stearns, and during the first

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:27.050
Reagan term he was the associate director
for economics of planning in the Office of

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.050
Management and Budget.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:33.910
He is currently a senior contributor at CNBC,
where he hosted for a long time The Kudlow

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:34.910
Report.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:37.679
He has a weekly syndicated radio show.

00:00:37.679 --> 00:00:44.489
He's a nationally syndicated columnist, contributing
editor at National Review, and he's CEO of

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:48.549
Kudlow &amp; Company LLC, an economic research
firm.

00:00:48.549 --> 00:00:55.219
Last, but far from least, he's a recipient
of numerous awards, most of them related to

00:00:55.219 --> 00:01:01.389
his philanthropic works, including the Bishop's
Humanitarian Award from the Catholic Charities

00:01:01.389 --> 00:01:06.760
of Brooklyn and Queens, the Humanitarian Award
from the Pregnancy Care Center of New Rochelle,

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:11.250
New York, and the Ambassadors for Mission
Award from the Pontifical Mission Societies

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.730
of the United States.

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:16.930
It's an honor really to introduce you to tonight
to speak on the economic debates going on

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:22.260
today, Mr. Larry Kudlow.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:30.910
Larry Kudlow: Thank you.

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:32.320
Appreciate it very much.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:38.770
It's a pleasure, honor to be here to help
Hillsdale in any way that I can.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:46.770
Let me begin, as I was instructed by the organizers,
to talk about the prospects of capitalism,

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.620
the prospects of capitalism.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:57.100
If you know me and if you've seen any of the
stuff I've done on the air you know I'm a

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.050
quintessential optimist, quintessential optimist.

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:07.351
I will you I think the prospects of capitalism
are pretty good, pretty good.

00:02:07.351 --> 00:02:10.530
They're not as good as I want them to be.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.810
We have a few issues.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.340
But they're pretty good.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:18.010
Let me make this caveat.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:25.420
If you take nothing else away from this talk
tonight, let me tell you this.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:39.510
If we want free markets, and limited government,
and sound money, and low taxes, and deregulation,

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:47.889
and market choice and competition, if want
to be part of the great traditional of Milton

00:02:47.889 --> 00:03:00.489
Friedman, or Friedrich Hyek, or Ludwig Von
Mesis, or Arthur Laffer, and many others,

00:03:00.489 --> 00:03:07.829
if want to be optimistic about the great country
of our ours and the great economy of ours,

00:03:07.829 --> 00:03:17.909
we, you and I, are going to have to fight
like hell to preserve it.

00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:25.290
They are not going to give it to us on a silver
platter.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:29.700
They are trying to gobble up as much as they
can.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:35.819
We kicked their butt in the last midterm election
but one never knows.

00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:40.340
Presidential politics is funny thing.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:46.829
A thought.

00:03:46.829 --> 00:03:55.989
Since World War II the US economy, generally
a free market economy, has grown at an average

00:03:55.989 --> 00:03:59.939
yearly rate of 3.3%.

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:05.199
That's from roughly 1947 to 2000.

00:04:05.199 --> 00:04:15.919
Did you know that over the last century, from
1900 to 2000, I think that's a hundred years,

00:04:15.919 --> 00:04:22.040
the US economy has grown at an annual rate
of 3.4%?

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:26.090
That's pretty good.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:36.260
I'm going to argue that there is no nation
in the history of history, no nation with

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:43.170
a free market capitalist experiment in the
history of history, who has ever produced

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:51.760
that kind of economic growth over that long
a period of time ever, ever.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:53.370
I will also say this.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:05:01.380
The great experiments of communism and Nazism
and fascism and totalitarianism, and I'm going

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:07.481
to talk more about totalitarianism in a few
minutes, all those great experiments, we're

00:05:07.481 --> 00:05:10.630
seeing a few of them now redux.

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:20.280
I thought when I was a young working for Reagan
I'd put a spike into Maynard Keynes and redistribution

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:21.440
and taxing the rich.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:27.220
I could have sworn I had him in the crypt,
but I was wrong.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:30.090
He's back.

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:34.860
This is a fight worth preserving, not only
because of its track record.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:39.860
It's a fight worth preserving for the generations
ahead of us, for the unborn.

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:43.740
That's why this is so important.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:53.190
As your president said, this is something
that will not come easily, constitutionally,

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:55.640
regutatorily, taxly, spendingly.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:59.170
This will not come easily.

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:09.980
However, however, however, I'm a great believer
that democracies breed free market capitalism.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:15.130
Now you can argue, and you'd be right, that
that is not always the case.

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:21.780
Now I will argue of course that democracies
do not breed any sort of state planning and

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:25.750
seizing the means of production, but you could
point to Europe and say they have democracies

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:27.690
but they don't really have much of form of
capitalism.

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:30.700
They actually have some form of capitalism
in Europe.

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:31.700
It's their own little design.

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:32.700
It doesn't work.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:36.640
They haven't grown in decades, but it's something.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:39.520
We've had a lot of influence down through
the years, particularly in the last three

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:48.200
decades with Reagan and Thatcher putting into
place the ideas of Milton Friedman and Friedrich

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:52.080
Hayek and the others I mentioned.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:53.880
We've had some influence in Asia.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.870
In fact I'll tell you a story in Asia.

00:06:56.870 --> 00:07:00.610
Now I'm not going to point to China as a democracy.

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:06.030
Will note however that the communist rules
have opened the economy up quite a bit.

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:11.710
They've liberalized it in the direction of
markets, in the direction of markets, in the

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:14.020
direction of ownership.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:18.510
So much so that I don't believe that the communists
will be able to remain in power.

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:23.520
Once you let that genie out of the bottle
it usually can never be put back in, but we'll

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:24.520
see.

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:29.410
On the other hand, hundreds of millions of
people have improved their lot in China and

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:32.300
so they now have a large, burgeoning middle
class.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:34.100
The same thing is true in India.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:41.890
India is a democracy, thankfully, and it has
a long history of socialist bureaucratic underperforms

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:48.050
the economy, but nonetheless, in the recent
decade or so, and maybe even more coming now

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:52.270
with a open market guy, a free market guy
running it, India, hundreds of millions of

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:56.820
people have come to enjoy middle class status.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:57.940
This is new.

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:00.810
They're basically following the Reagan-Thatcher
model.

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:03.240
That's what they're doing.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:10.240
There's a long tradition of that in the United
States and in the English-seapking countries.

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:11.340
That's what freedom will do.

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:13.270
I have had my disagreements.

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:20.500
I do a lot of service for the Catholic Church,
quite true.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:27.020
Far be it from me to criticize the pope, who
doesn't share my zest for free market capitalism.

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:34.139
That's as strong as I'm going to get on that
subject.

00:08:34.139 --> 00:08:35.139
I did get a call.

00:08:35.139 --> 00:08:40.000
It's important if you're a Catholic to get
a call.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:46.590
I got a call from the Archbishop of New York,
Cardinal Dolan.

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:48.010
Never met the guy before.

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:53.440
I sat down with a few of his people and some
big church people.

00:08:53.440 --> 00:09:01.890
We were talking about what to do with some
of the pope's proclamations about free market

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:02.890
capitalism.

00:09:02.890 --> 00:09:09.050
One guy in particular said, "You know eminence,
we're trying to raise 2-300 million dollars

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:13.950
here to fix the roof of the church," which
is a noble enterprise, St. Patrick's Church.

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:14.990
It's a landmark.

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:17.140
It's very important, and people worship there.

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:20.550
Don't want the roof to fall on their head.

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:27.860
This guy said, "It's very hard if the pope
keeps making these proclamations against rich

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:33.370
people who are here to provide the 2 or 300
million dollars to fix your roof."

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:38.740
By the way, I'm not a theoretician as it must
be apparent.

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:43.370
I see life in very practical, real world terms.

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:45.210
He said, "I'm bringing Kudlow in here.

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:50.800
I'm want you to meet Larry Kudlow, who believes
in free market capitalism, and he'd like to

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:52.360
talk to you."

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:54.500
I said, "Eminence, look.

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:57.110
I want you to write an op-ed piece.

00:09:57.110 --> 00:10:03.930
I'm not here to suggest that you're going
to attack the holy father, but I am here to

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:11.190
suggest that we can write you an op-ed piece
that will provide you a little more balance

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:12.260
in the story."

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:21.130
I took out a graph, a chart, from the World
Bank, which is a left-wing organization.

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:31.360
The chart basically says, with great clarity,
over the last three decades, which has been

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:40.800
the rising tide of the spread of free market
capitalism around the world, the rate of abject

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:52.400
dollar a day poverty has fallen by 80%, 80%.

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:59.240
That number is so big and so convincing of
the merits of free market capitalism that

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:05.190
it deserves at least some papal attention.

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:06.660
Now I'm going to leave that alone.

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:09.220
I'm not here to talk to you about the Catholic
Church.

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:11.870
I could tell you jokes and I'm not going to
go there.

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:19.510
It's much too serious an evening tonight,
but I'm proud of it and it's working.

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:25.980
Look, we've had periods of time when the country
has moved to the left, but the good news is

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:28.330
it never really stays to the left.

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:29.760
Now you may disagree with that.

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:30.760
I appreciate that.

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:38.060
We could probably debate some of that but
that is my particular view.

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:42.370
Somehow over the last hundred years we've
grown at almost 3.5% per year.

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:43.690
I think that's remarkable.

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:49.860
I was chatting on air a week or two ago with
my pal Glenn Beck.

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:53.500
He was interviewing me on one thing or another.

00:11:53.500 --> 00:12:00.690
He fancies himself a classical liberal, which
is kind of a cool terms, a classical liberal.

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:04.000
I love to take their language away for them.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.130
I always have.

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:07.130
Thank you.

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:08.130
What's a classical liberal?

00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:10.279
We were kind of going at this.

00:12:10.279 --> 00:12:12.490
You're a classical ... I said, "Yeah, it's
Hayek, it's Milton Friedman."

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:15.370
I'd just think yeah, you know what?

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:23.270
If I had lived in the 19th century I would
have been a Gladstonian.

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:31.710
I would have been a follower of William Gladstone,
four-time liberal prime minister of Britain.

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:34.700
What did he believe in?

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:39.390
It turns out a couple of guys in The Economist
had actually written a piece.

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:44.870
I went and googled it up and they call it
Gladstonian republicanism and said the GOP

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:46.470
should learn from Gladstone.

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:53.540
It was 150 years ago, whatever, but he actually
cut the size of the British government by

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:59.070
a third during a period when their economy
expanded by 50%.

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:02.000
Britain ruled the waves and had the greatest
economy in the world.

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:08.420
He's slashing government, virtually abolishing
taxes, virtually abolishing tariffs, and virtually

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:09.920
abolishing regulations.

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:17.970
Actually, the liberal in our welfare big government
entitlement terms was Disraeli.

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:20.790
He was supposed to be the conservative.

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:26.230
Glenn Beck and I talked about the need for
classical liberalism to return.

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:28.200
That's what I'm saying to you this evening.

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:29.810
We can do this.

00:13:29.810 --> 00:13:31.050
We know what we're doing.

00:13:31.050 --> 00:13:35.890
If we're well rooted in the theory, if we
know the difference between right and wrong,

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:38.190
we can go after the bad guys.

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:40.140
Look at what we've done.

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:44.680
Don't talk to me about a couple bad years
of stagnant middle class wages.

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:45.680
I get that.

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:50.380
By the way, my pal Alan Reynolds just did
a study and said the top 1% has lost money

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:52.350
in the whole bunch of last years too.

00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:54.310
You can't have a healthy economy without rich
people.

00:13:54.310 --> 00:13:55.770
Can I just say that?

00:13:55.770 --> 00:14:00.710
You cannot have a healthy economy without
someone succeeding.

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:07.670
Someone has to succeed in order to create
wealth.

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:09.760
I don't know.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:16.910
We had dinner with President Obama just before
the inauguration at George Will's house.

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:19.010
It's a tradition because he's George Will.

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:24.940
There were nine or ten of us conservative
yappers.

00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:25.940
We met the president.

00:14:25.940 --> 00:14:32.430
It was charming and sort of humorous, well
turned out.

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:41.020
He was humorous until Krauthammer went at
him and then he kind of lost his sense of

00:14:41.020 --> 00:14:42.020
humor.

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:44.740
I asked about marginal tax rates.

00:14:44.740 --> 00:14:47.940
He was talking 40s on the way to 50.

00:14:47.940 --> 00:14:53.209
Bill Kristol or somebody asked him about healthcare
and he was talking about his grandmother's

00:14:53.209 --> 00:14:56.260
hip or something and how the state took care
of it.

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:59.270
I knew we were in for trouble.

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:01.320
I knew right then and there.

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:04.150
But look, it's not stopping progress.

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:05.670
It's not stopping progress.

00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:09.120
It's weakened it, it's slowed it, but it's
not stopped it.

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:14.470
The whole issue of freedom and rewarding success
is never going to be stopped.

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:18.660
It is in the very roots of democracy, the
very roots of democracy, the very roots of

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:22.180
constitutional rule.

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:23.630
I have a simple definition.

00:15:23.630 --> 00:15:26.040
I want lower marginal tax rates.

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:29.540
Hell, I'd take a flat tax any day of the week.

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:31.220
I want government cut to the bone.

00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:34.670
I want regulations as light as possible.

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:38.980
I want sound money, a reliable dollar, so
people can invest here.

00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:41.700
Of course I want the rule of law.

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:46.230
The president has violated pretty much all
of those things, but there is some good news.

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:51.029
As his term comes to an end I notice the dollar
is going up.

00:15:51.029 --> 00:15:53.200
This is good.

00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:57.110
As many of us have argued for many years,
as the dollar goes up the price of oil goes

00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:58.110
down.

00:15:58.110 --> 00:15:59.110
This is also good.

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:03.690
This is helping middle class people, because
their dollars will be worth more.

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:08.580
All commodities are going down, and food prices
are going down along with energy and gasoline

00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:12.190
prices.

00:16:12.190 --> 00:16:19.450
Even though the president encouraged the Fed
to create 4.5 trillion new dollars in their

00:16:19.450 --> 00:16:27.080
balance sheet and hates fossil fuel, we are
benefiting because the Federal Reserve has

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:33.529
finally stopped printing money and fossil
fuel has created one of the technological

00:16:33.529 --> 00:16:38.209
energy revolutions in our history, so I'll
prices are closer to 50 than 150.

00:16:38.209 --> 00:16:41.899
I don't mind if the president takes credit
for that.

00:16:41.899 --> 00:16:44.180
The presidents do but he didn't have a bloody
thing to do with it.

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:50.149
It's just happening because we are basically
still a free market democracy and a free market

00:16:50.149 --> 00:16:51.149
economy.

00:16:51.149 --> 00:16:56.370
Guys like Harold Hamm going up to North Dakota
and saying I can frack, I can do horizontal

00:16:56.370 --> 00:16:58.380
drilling, nobody believed him.

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:02.370
He did it anyway and that's still the great
spirit of free market capitalism.

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:03.370
It is alive and well.

00:17:03.370 --> 00:17:08.520
Look, in our lifetime we've gone from industry
to technology and information and the internet,

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:10.079
in our lifetime.

00:17:10.079 --> 00:17:14.049
You know how important that is?

00:17:14.049 --> 00:17:15.049
We led the we.

00:17:15.049 --> 00:17:17.770
We, the US, led the way.

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:20.010
You know why?

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:23.410
Because we were free to do so.

00:17:23.410 --> 00:17:25.980
You know what else?

00:17:25.980 --> 00:17:35.730
People who led this revolution were able to
get rewards for their successful risk talking

00:17:35.730 --> 00:17:37.820
and entrepreneurship.

00:17:37.820 --> 00:17:40.960
That's what happened.

00:17:40.960 --> 00:17:45.950
As long as that model survives ... My pal
Arthur Brooks who runs the American Enterprise

00:17:45.950 --> 00:17:53.300
Institute writes about this wonderful stuff,
the immorality of punishing success and the

00:17:53.300 --> 00:17:54.929
morality of rewarding success.

00:17:54.929 --> 00:18:01.740
As you know from the Declaration of Independence,
our creator endowed us with inalienable rights

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:05.030
of life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.

00:18:05.030 --> 00:18:10.460
It is the pursuit of happiness part that interests
me a lot.

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:11.460
Life?

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:12.460
Yes.

00:18:12.460 --> 00:18:13.460
Liberty?

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:14.460
Yes.

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:20.760
Pursuit of happiness means you are free to
fail or succeed.

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:26.750
In so doing you will produce great things
that nobody believed possible.

00:18:26.750 --> 00:18:30.390
I don't ever, ever want to lose that freedom.

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:36.700
That's why I say to you tonight, the outlook
for free market capitalism's pretty good but

00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:38.860
you're going to have to fight like hell.

00:18:38.860 --> 00:18:45.720
They're going to come at us in the next election
and then the next midterm election.

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:50.000
We thought we drive a spike into Keynesianism
and redistribution 30 years ago.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:56.590
We did not, so we'll just keep fighting away
with good arguments, persuasive arguments,

00:18:56.590 --> 00:18:59.970
numerical arguments, numerical arguments.

00:18:59.970 --> 00:19:01.710
I think the tide is turning.

00:19:01.710 --> 00:19:03.290
It's moving in our direction.

00:19:03.290 --> 00:19:12.030
We have much more work to do but I notice,
along with the oil drop and the dollar rise,

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:15.730
jobs are getting better, wages are picking
up.

00:19:15.730 --> 00:19:19.049
This is because the instinct of our economy
is to grow.

00:19:19.049 --> 00:19:24.820
Business is very resilient despite the fact
that you have an anti-business president,

00:19:24.820 --> 00:19:26.220
breathtakingly anti-business president.

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:28.390
I'll give you an example of this.

00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:29.390
I'm an economist.

00:19:29.390 --> 00:19:32.100
I won't quote constitutional stuff.

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:34.550
The president says he wants corporate tax
reform.

00:19:34.550 --> 00:19:38.610
My belief right now is that single most important
thing that would help the American economy

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:44.540
in the short run would be slashing of the
corporate tax rate and an end to the double

00:19:44.540 --> 00:19:47.670
taxation of foreign profits parked overseas.

00:19:47.670 --> 00:19:49.830
Single best thing we could do.

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:55.630
If you had that big tax cut, marginal tax
rate cut, and an improving dollar, that would

00:19:55.630 --> 00:20:01.790
give the Fed cover to do what it needs to
do to try to get its affairs back in order

00:20:01.790 --> 00:20:03.390
so they can write a good check again.

00:20:03.390 --> 00:20:06.650
Now, Obama says, "I'm for corporate tax reform."

00:20:06.650 --> 00:20:13.000
Then he goes to the State of Union message
and then he puts out his budget.

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:19.720
He says, "I don't really like those multinationals
that send the money abroad.

00:20:19.720 --> 00:20:20.940
They're unpatriotic.

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:21.940
Here's the deal.

00:20:21.940 --> 00:20:24.320
If they bring their money home ... " It's
about two trillion dollars worth.

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:25.840
Two trillion's a lot of money.

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:27.860
The whole economy is only about 17 trillion.

00:20:27.860 --> 00:20:29.260
"They bring their money home.

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:37.600
I will tax them at 19% for the first year,
and then 14% thereafter."

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:40.600
I think that's right, or is it 14% and 9%.

00:20:40.600 --> 00:20:41.800
Or, 19% and 14%.

00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:42.800
That's a lousy deal.

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:44.470
They can do that now.

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:47.520
The way the system works you pay about 25%
overseas.

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:48.530
We're 35.

00:20:48.530 --> 00:20:50.540
You pay 25% overseas.

00:20:50.540 --> 00:20:56.000
You get to deduct that from your 35% but you
still have to pay 10% if you bring the money

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:57.500
home, so it's taxed twice.

00:20:57.500 --> 00:21:01.390
Obama says, "I'm going to give you a big break
and tax you at 14%."

00:21:01.390 --> 00:21:02.700
Ten is as high as they can go.

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:04.790
That's the break even point.

00:21:04.790 --> 00:21:10.780
The current system, as bad as it is, is actually
better than the one he's proposing, and he

00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:14.330
says he's for corporate tax reform.

00:21:14.330 --> 00:21:15.960
I don't know what I think about that.

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:20.580
He must think, to use the words of one of
his advisors, that Americans are stupid.

00:21:20.580 --> 00:21:22.169
Well we are not.

00:21:22.169 --> 00:21:26.720
We are not, and my pal and yours Paul Ryan
is going to go after that.

00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:31.980
We'll see if we can't do better, and hopefully
it will an election that will.

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:35.660
Thank you on behalf of Paul, whom I've known
for many years.

00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:36.790
Thank you.

00:21:36.790 --> 00:21:39.059
Let me raise a couple of other points.

00:21:39.059 --> 00:21:41.500
Then I want to take your questions.

00:21:41.500 --> 00:21:42.500
I don't get out much.

00:21:42.500 --> 00:21:46.260
I'm dying to hear what's on your mind.

00:21:46.260 --> 00:21:51.780
As much of a believer of economic incentives
and free markets and choice as I am, as much

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:59.490
as I acknowledge the need to roll back Obamacare
and substitute market choice and competition,

00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:04.400
as much as I want to reform the entitlement
state, as much as I want to reward success

00:22:04.400 --> 00:22:06.750
rather than punish it, I want to raise two
issues.

00:22:06.750 --> 00:22:09.930
They're economic, yes, but they're not economic.

00:22:09.930 --> 00:22:15.049
One of them is the issue of marriage, marriage.

00:22:15.049 --> 00:22:16.710
Stay with me on this.

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:19.720
Stay with me on this.

00:22:19.720 --> 00:22:28.059
Every study shows, left of center, right of
center, virtually every study shows that married

00:22:28.059 --> 00:22:38.500
people have higher incomes and greater wealth
than unmarried people.

00:22:38.500 --> 00:22:44.049
By the way, as a practical guy who's had his
ups and downs in life I can just say to you

00:22:44.049 --> 00:22:49.059
if you are divorced you can get remarried
for a long time.

00:22:49.059 --> 00:22:50.059
It will stick.

00:22:50.059 --> 00:22:53.140
Mine's 27 years and growing.

00:22:53.140 --> 00:23:03.030
They also show that probably the single biggest
cause of poverty is broken families, unmarried

00:23:03.030 --> 00:23:04.480
and broken families.

00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:13.400
Third, they show that the government is subsidizing
broken families, fatherless families.

00:23:13.400 --> 00:23:17.960
In fact, the government is even subsidizing
situations where two people live under the

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:19.730
same roof but neither of them work.

00:23:19.730 --> 00:23:25.240
They have kids and the subsidizes that.

00:23:25.240 --> 00:23:32.940
What I'm here to say, and it's a theme I've
been developing for many months, the culture

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:43.530
of marriage is one of the most important economic
solutions possible, as well as an inherently

00:23:43.530 --> 00:23:48.880
moral solution as well.

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:56.480
We should do everything we can, everything
we can, whether it's cultural or tax or economic

00:23:56.480 --> 00:23:59.049
or entitlement, to change this.

00:23:59.049 --> 00:24:07.130
I want every presidential candidate to tackle
the issue of marriage and family breakup,

00:24:07.130 --> 00:24:10.049
and the federal government's role in that.

00:24:10.049 --> 00:24:14.289
It turns out the government plays an important
role in that.

00:24:14.289 --> 00:24:19.799
The last democrat I worked for ... Woops,
I'm an ex-democrat.

00:24:19.799 --> 00:24:21.320
Lot of Reagan people were ex-democrats.

00:24:21.320 --> 00:24:25.850
Last democrat I worked for is Senator Pat
Moynihan who wrote a study about this regarding

00:24:25.850 --> 00:24:28.010
black families and their pathologies.

00:24:28.010 --> 00:24:29.830
He wrote that in the mid-60s.

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:34.000
He was completely trashed, trashed by the
academic institutions.

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:38.100
He was completely and utterly right, completely
and utterly right.

00:24:38.100 --> 00:24:40.470
Read Jason Riley in the Wall Street Journal.

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:42.200
Now he's gone over to the Manhattan Institute.

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:44.000
He was on my radio show this past week.

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:48.860
He wrote a brilliant op-ed piece in the Wall
Street Journal about this very fact, about

00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:49.860
this very fact.

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:51.059
It isn't only a black problem.

00:24:51.059 --> 00:24:56.290
It has become a white problem as Charles Murray
has taught us, and it has been a brown problem.

00:24:56.290 --> 00:25:00.650
In other words, it's just a problem.

00:25:00.650 --> 00:25:04.309
We need more, healthier, longer marriages.

00:25:04.309 --> 00:25:08.730
For the kids, yes, and for society.

00:25:08.730 --> 00:25:10.610
On this point I'll just close.

00:25:10.610 --> 00:25:12.669
One last point: I call it Kudlow 101.

00:25:12.669 --> 00:25:14.350
Very simple.

00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:18.710
If you're a child of a broken family, it's
predictable that you are going to fail and

00:25:18.710 --> 00:25:23.941
may be enmeshed in poverty and have no upward
social mobility, and I hate that.

00:25:23.941 --> 00:25:26.340
I hate the thought of that.

00:25:26.340 --> 00:25:28.289
I mentor.

00:25:28.289 --> 00:25:29.700
I mentor and I sponsor.

00:25:29.700 --> 00:25:30.900
I'm in 12-step programs.

00:25:30.900 --> 00:25:33.610
I'm in community programs.

00:25:33.610 --> 00:25:36.880
First, go to school.

00:25:36.880 --> 00:25:38.870
Go to school every day.

00:25:38.870 --> 00:25:42.670
Get your high school diploma and then go on
a bit.

00:25:42.670 --> 00:25:45.020
I don't care if you go to a four-year college
or not.

00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:48.490
Get a technical degree, learn to fix things.

00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:50.850
There's great money in that.

00:25:50.850 --> 00:25:51.850
Whatever suits you.

00:25:51.850 --> 00:25:52.900
You don't have to go to Princeton.

00:25:52.900 --> 00:25:54.070
I went to Princeton.

00:25:54.070 --> 00:25:56.990
By the way, I just want to digress for one
minute.

00:25:56.990 --> 00:26:01.300
It is my great hope that the president of
the United States will not have an Ivy League

00:26:01.300 --> 00:26:02.539
degree.

00:26:02.539 --> 00:26:07.510
It's my great hope.

00:26:07.510 --> 00:26:14.559
I am already agitating for a law ... My pal
Rand Paul and others want to have rules to

00:26:14.559 --> 00:26:15.640
audit the Federal Reserve.

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:17.919
I have a better one for the Federal Reserve.

00:26:17.919 --> 00:26:26.440
Pass a law that says any Ivy League economist
with an econometric model is automatically

00:26:26.440 --> 00:26:29.660
disqualified for holding the post.

00:26:29.660 --> 00:26:31.130
You think I'm kidding?

00:26:31.130 --> 00:26:32.130
I'm not.

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:36.030
I've been there and I've done that, but I
digress.

00:26:36.030 --> 00:26:39.840
I want the kid to go to school and get some
skills.

00:26:39.840 --> 00:26:44.770
Then I want he or she to get a job, a job.

00:26:44.770 --> 00:26:54.049
I want them to hold the job for a bunch of
years like we did, like we did.

00:26:54.049 --> 00:26:55.110
You start at the bottom of the ladder.

00:26:55.110 --> 00:26:56.110
You work your way up.

00:26:56.110 --> 00:26:57.289
That's a very American notion.

00:26:57.289 --> 00:26:59.600
I don't want to ever lose that.

00:26:59.600 --> 00:27:01.840
That is as precious as free market capitalism.

00:27:01.840 --> 00:27:05.760
Indeed, it is a cornerstone of free market
capitalism.

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:07.049
Hold onto a job.

00:27:07.049 --> 00:27:11.370
Then start dating.

00:27:11.370 --> 00:27:19.250
Now my wife calls it doing research.

00:27:19.250 --> 00:27:25.990
Living together, jumping in the bed together,
we tried that.

00:27:25.990 --> 00:27:27.740
Sometimes it works.

00:27:27.740 --> 00:27:30.090
More than 50% of the time it doesn't work.

00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:32.790
Just get to know each other.

00:27:32.790 --> 00:27:33.850
Then get married.

00:27:33.850 --> 00:27:39.960
Then get married and be married for several
years with a job for a bunch of jobs, and

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:45.300
then have the kid.

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:50.080
The trouble with today's story is it's being
done backwards.

00:27:50.080 --> 00:27:57.330
They have the kid first before the marriage,
before the job, before the education, and

00:27:57.330 --> 00:27:59.770
it is most regrettable.

00:27:59.770 --> 00:28:01.590
This has to be changed.

00:28:01.590 --> 00:28:08.860
To me as a guy who has fought for economic
freedom, and lower marginal tax rates, and

00:28:08.860 --> 00:28:13.919
dregulation, and a sound dollar my entire
life, this marriage thing is looming larger

00:28:13.919 --> 00:28:14.919
and larger.

00:28:14.919 --> 00:28:16.420
The more I read, the more I'm convinced.

00:28:16.420 --> 00:28:17.850
That's why I present it to you.

00:28:17.850 --> 00:28:22.419
My final point this evening is on international
policy.

00:28:22.419 --> 00:28:27.710
This is terribly important, too, as you know.

00:28:27.710 --> 00:28:33.160
I was just reading Annelise Anderson's book.

00:28:33.160 --> 00:28:35.490
Her dear husband, these are dear friends of
mine.

00:28:35.490 --> 00:28:40.230
Marty just passed away but they put out this
great book about Reagan's great decisions.

00:28:40.230 --> 00:28:46.250
Reagan used to always argue weakness at home
leads to weakness abroad, but strength at

00:28:46.250 --> 00:28:48.210
home leads to strength abroad.

00:28:48.210 --> 00:28:54.659
When your economy is solid at home you have
the resources to produce the natural security

00:28:54.659 --> 00:28:57.289
and meet your commitments.

00:28:57.289 --> 00:29:01.530
If you draw lines in the sand, then you meet
that commitment.

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:06.550
Unfortunately, today we have a soft economy.

00:29:06.550 --> 00:29:08.980
We're not generating the resources.

00:29:08.980 --> 00:29:13.179
Our friends don't respect us and our enemies
don't fear us.

00:29:13.179 --> 00:29:21.330
This is a terrible situation, but it can be
fixed, it can be fixed, as long as we understand

00:29:21.330 --> 00:29:23.800
the stakes are very high.

00:29:23.800 --> 00:29:26.140
That's all I'm getting at.

00:29:26.140 --> 00:29:30.530
My friend and frequent guest on the Kudlow
Report and on the radio show, General Jack

00:29:30.530 --> 00:29:34.690
Keane, started me on this.

00:29:34.690 --> 00:29:42.010
He compared this battle with the Islamic jihadist
radicals and the rest of the world as a battle

00:29:42.010 --> 00:29:50.539
reminiscent of the battle between freedom
and communism in the last century.

00:29:50.539 --> 00:29:55.360
I just raised it up and said, "General, can
I just call this a battle between freedom

00:29:55.360 --> 00:29:57.390
and totalitarianism?"

00:29:57.390 --> 00:29:59.330
That's what this is.

00:29:59.330 --> 00:30:02.220
It's not just about one country or one border.

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:09.620
This is about a whole movement that wishes
to destroy us, our way of life, and our principles,

00:30:09.620 --> 00:30:12.110
and our constitutions, and everything else.

00:30:12.110 --> 00:30:13.600
We can't allow this to happen.

00:30:13.600 --> 00:30:17.520
We need a plan but we need resolve.

00:30:17.520 --> 00:30:22.660
We need an economy and we need resolve, and
we need a plan.

00:30:22.660 --> 00:30:27.770
I will recommend to you a book written by
the mayor of London, Boris Johnson.

00:30:27.770 --> 00:30:31.159
It's called the Churchill Factor.

00:30:31.159 --> 00:30:32.799
Book's an easy read.

00:30:32.799 --> 00:30:40.620
Mr. Johnson reminds us, says Churchill was
the most important figure in the 20th century,

00:30:40.620 --> 00:30:43.700
the most important figure in the 20th century.

00:30:43.700 --> 00:30:44.700
Why?

00:30:44.700 --> 00:30:55.210
Because when Britain and the world needed
him, he was there to fight totalitarianism.

00:30:55.210 --> 00:30:56.270
We didn't know at that point.

00:30:56.270 --> 00:30:59.940
It looked like Nazism but it could have been
communism, etc. etc.

00:30:59.940 --> 00:31:02.650
If he didn't fight it, nobody else would.

00:31:02.650 --> 00:31:09.179
Meaning no disrespect, but FDR and the USA
wasn't there politically yet.

00:31:09.179 --> 00:31:14.830
Meaning a lot of disrespect, the leaders of
Churchills own party were a bunch of appeasing

00:31:14.830 --> 00:31:16.409
wusses.

00:31:16.409 --> 00:31:22.920
On the other hand, the nobility of Britain,
half of them were Nazi sympathizers.

00:31:22.920 --> 00:31:27.240
They figured Hitler might come in, put his
guy in charge nominally but they would continue

00:31:27.240 --> 00:31:29.830
to live the great life that they thought they
were living.

00:31:29.830 --> 00:31:31.640
Wrong, wrong.

00:31:31.640 --> 00:31:35.850
We should never kid ourselves about the evils
of totalitarianism ever.

00:31:35.850 --> 00:31:42.720
God bless there was Winston Churchill, a guy
who had seen bullets in several wars who understand.

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:49.470
He fought and he stood up and he had a lot
of courage.

00:31:49.470 --> 00:31:51.620
I don't know who the Churchill is today.

00:31:51.620 --> 00:31:53.790
I don't know that.

00:31:53.790 --> 00:31:55.419
Not smart enough to know.

00:31:55.419 --> 00:32:04.560
I know that there are a bunch of very smart,
conscionable, free market-oriented Republican

00:32:04.560 --> 00:32:12.590
governors who are going to fight it out for
the nomination to be president, and I think

00:32:12.590 --> 00:32:13.590
that's good.

00:32:13.590 --> 00:32:17.290
As somebody who believes in free market competition
I say let the games begin.

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:22.200
I want to see the debates, etc. etc.

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:32.900
Alls I ask is that the next leader take account
of history lessons both recent and in the

00:32:32.900 --> 00:32:34.080
past.

00:32:34.080 --> 00:32:42.020
We can grow this economy at 3.5 to 4% like
we have for the last hundred years if we reward

00:32:42.020 --> 00:32:48.190
success and choose free market choice over
government statism and entitlements.

00:32:48.190 --> 00:32:49.190
That's one point.

00:32:49.190 --> 00:32:57.710
We can achieve 3.5 to 4% growth if we pull
back on regulations and if we lighten the

00:32:57.710 --> 00:33:00.059
tax burden, and we keep the dollar sound.

00:33:00.059 --> 00:33:01.659
We can do this.

00:33:01.659 --> 00:33:05.620
If we observe constitutional rules of law
and order we can do this.

00:33:05.620 --> 00:33:08.270
We know how to do this.

00:33:08.270 --> 00:33:16.320
If we have an ear and a sense of the importance
of a conservative, traditional cultural of

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:19.660
marriage, we can do this.

00:33:19.660 --> 00:33:25.730
Then if we have, finally, a sense of the importance
of the battle that is going on internationally

00:33:25.730 --> 00:33:32.450
between freedom and totalitarianism, we can
do this.

00:33:32.450 --> 00:33:38.730
My message to you at the end of this talk,
as it was in the beginning, I'm an optimist,

00:33:38.730 --> 00:33:45.690
but you and I must fight like hell in order
to win.

00:33:45.690 --> 00:34:05.029
Thank you for inviting me.

00:34:05.029 --> 00:34:15.230
You're way too kind.

00:34:15.230 --> 00:34:16.690
I'm most grateful.

00:34:16.690 --> 00:34:25.130
Nothing I've said that your president wasn't
talking about in his own way and will continue.

00:34:25.130 --> 00:34:29.310
It's such an honor for me to be able to address
Hillsdale College.

00:34:29.310 --> 00:34:35.540
With permission from our officials and moderators
and what not I would love to take a few questions.

00:34:35.540 --> 00:34:37.950
Like I say, I don't get out much.

00:34:37.950 --> 00:34:39.130
This is a hell of a crowd.

00:34:39.130 --> 00:34:44.470
I don't think I've ever seen so many free
market conservatives under one roof before,

00:34:44.470 --> 00:34:46.260
so God bless you.

00:34:46.260 --> 00:34:51.409
If I had it to do over again I'd swap my Princeton
degree for yours, believe me.

00:34:51.409 --> 00:34:54.630
I will do penance by going out there and actually
seeing Hillsdale.

00:34:54.630 --> 00:34:59.930
I'm anxious to actually see it and meet the
kids, meet the kids who are the most important.

00:34:59.930 --> 00:35:03.400
Anyway, please if you have a question or a
disagreement go right ahead.

00:35:03.400 --> 00:35:04.400
Yes sir.

00:35:04.400 --> 00:35:05.910
Speaker 3: Your comments on marriage were
good but there is some issues there sometimes.

00:35:05.910 --> 00:35:07.000
Larry Kudlow: I'm sorry, I can't hear you,
sir.

00:35:07.000 --> 00:35:08.000
Speaker 3: Is that better?

00:35:08.000 --> 00:35:09.000
Hello?

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:10.000
Everyone can hear me except you, sir.

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:11.000
Anyway, your comments on marriage were good.

00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:12.000
Larry Kudlow: Thank you.

00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:13.000
Speaker 3: But, there's other issues sometimes.

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:14.000
You mentioned that fellow Mr. Hamm, right?

00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:15.000
Larry Kudlow: Right.

00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:20.780
Speaker 3: He has some issues with divorce.

00:35:20.780 --> 00:35:36.000
I'm being a little facetious but the thing
is not always perfect.

00:35:36.000 --> 00:36:02.100
I guess my point is, it's kind of goofy but
this is really travesties of justice I that

00:36:02.100 --> 00:36:03.100
arena also.

00:36:03.100 --> 00:36:04.130
Larry Kudlow: I suppose so.

00:36:04.130 --> 00:36:06.620
I don't know.

00:36:06.620 --> 00:36:10.150
Like I say, it's about progress, not perfection.

00:36:10.150 --> 00:36:17.930
If you swing at the ball and hit a ground
out, you can come back again, get a base it.

00:36:17.930 --> 00:36:19.340
You know what I mean.

00:36:19.340 --> 00:36:20.340
You know what I mean.

00:36:20.340 --> 00:36:21.990
You all know what I mean.

00:36:21.990 --> 00:36:25.530
I'm not here to morally judge anybody, lord
knows.

00:36:25.530 --> 00:36:32.260
You're talking to a Catholic convert who with
the help of God completely changed his life

00:36:32.260 --> 00:36:34.950
20 years ago.

00:36:34.950 --> 00:36:39.720
I'm not here to take your inventory.

00:36:39.720 --> 00:36:49.090
All I'm saying is that in the economic zone,
in the economic sphere, it's not just about

00:36:49.090 --> 00:36:51.780
tax cuts and regulations.

00:36:51.780 --> 00:37:01.930
I'm expressing my worry that we are creating
permanent poverty pockets and a lack of upward

00:37:01.930 --> 00:37:03.570
social mobility.

00:37:03.570 --> 00:37:10.970
I'm saying to you that the evidence, the data,
are now coming in an overwhelming manner that

00:37:10.970 --> 00:37:15.290
the decline of the culture of marriage and
the decline of marriages, and the increase

00:37:15.290 --> 00:37:27.349
in family breakup, has had a devastating effect
on the kids, on the families, and on our economy.

00:37:27.349 --> 00:37:32.890
That's what it's saying.

00:37:32.890 --> 00:37:50.520
Speaker 4: Mr. Kudlow, how is it that the
theories of Samuelson and others on dealing

00:37:50.520 --> 00:37:57.210
with the deficits and the way to grow the
economy, which I thought you nailed down copy

00:37:57.210 --> 00:38:07.790
before, how is it they can still keep coming
to the fore, and what method could you propose

00:38:07.790 --> 00:38:12.270
for economists to develop a definitive program
for what grows the economy and what doesn't

00:38:12.270 --> 00:38:24.250
to the level it's on sort of a scientific
basis rather than an emotional basis.

00:38:24.250 --> 00:38:27.619
Larry Kudlow: It's a great question, sir.

00:38:27.619 --> 00:38:32.609
It's a very important question.

00:38:32.609 --> 00:38:38.740
People with greater economic credentials than
I have puzzled with this question.

00:38:38.740 --> 00:38:40.800
I would argue the evidence is clear.

00:38:40.800 --> 00:38:42.770
You're implying you agree with that.

00:38:42.770 --> 00:38:44.200
The evidence is clear.

00:38:44.200 --> 00:38:48.260
Let me just go back one second.

00:38:48.260 --> 00:38:55.270
I'm working with a co-author to write a book
about John F. Kennedy's tax cuts, as you know

00:38:55.270 --> 00:38:58.600
that Kennedy was really the first post-world
war supply sider.

00:38:58.600 --> 00:39:01.099
He lowered marginal tax rates.

00:39:01.099 --> 00:39:08.590
He cut taxes for rich people, middle income
people, corporations, capital gains, dividends.

00:39:08.590 --> 00:39:13.850
He was the last Democrat really to do so except
Bill Clinton, who was pushed into it by Newt

00:39:13.850 --> 00:39:16.730
Gingrich, but I'll give Clinton credit, too.

00:39:16.730 --> 00:39:19.720
Anyway, we had a boom in the 60s.

00:39:19.720 --> 00:39:25.390
Before we destroyed the dollar and the land,
we had a boom, one of the best booms in American

00:39:25.390 --> 00:39:27.460
history.

00:39:27.460 --> 00:39:31.570
In the 1980s we lowered marginal tax rates,
limited government.

00:39:31.570 --> 00:39:33.670
By the way, Kennedy was a stickler for the
budget.

00:39:33.670 --> 00:39:34.990
He was a budget restraint guy.

00:39:34.990 --> 00:39:39.450
It may not have been everything about him
that we like and love but on these issues

00:39:39.450 --> 00:39:44.650
he got ... By the way, he insisted that the
dollar stay as good as gold.

00:39:44.650 --> 00:39:45.670
My kind of guy.

00:39:45.670 --> 00:39:48.670
Sorry I never met him.

00:39:48.670 --> 00:39:53.839
Similar principles were laid down in the 80s
and similar principles were laid down in the

00:39:53.839 --> 00:39:54.839
90s.

00:39:54.839 --> 00:39:57.470
This is all in our lifetime.

00:39:57.470 --> 00:40:00.830
We had huge booms.

00:40:00.830 --> 00:40:07.950
I always love it when my friends on the left
tell me how bad lowering marginal tax rates

00:40:07.950 --> 00:40:09.060
is.

00:40:09.060 --> 00:40:16.700
Bill Clinton, he raised the rate from 31 to
40, yeah, but he also lowered the capital

00:40:16.700 --> 00:40:23.410
gains rate from 28 to 20 and reformed welfare
and passed a free trade bill, had Graham-Rudman,

00:40:23.410 --> 00:40:30.750
a bunch of other budget restraints that Gingrich
and the Republicans worked with him on.

00:40:30.750 --> 00:40:37.550
My pal and I Steve Moore one night were on
the radio together and in the 80s and 90s,

00:40:37.550 --> 00:40:40.760
which Obama has trashed ... He said none of
that stuff worked.

00:40:40.760 --> 00:40:42.460
He calls it all trickle down.

00:40:42.460 --> 00:40:54.829
In the 80s and 90s the United States created
almost 50 million new jobs in 20 years.

00:40:54.829 --> 00:41:01.609
The expansion of our economy was greater than
the total gross domestic product of any European

00:41:01.609 --> 00:41:03.780
country during that period.

00:41:03.780 --> 00:41:11.000
In fact, the expansion of our GDP is almost
greater than the whole size of the European

00:41:11.000 --> 00:41:12.170
Union.

00:41:12.170 --> 00:41:18.940
We did this in two decades under sound policies,
what I will simply call free market economics,

00:41:18.940 --> 00:41:26.010
Gladstonian liberalism, Milton Friedman, whatever,
Friedrich Hayek.

00:41:26.010 --> 00:41:28.850
Obama actually calls it all trickle down.

00:41:28.850 --> 00:41:39.910
Now, the 1970s, where we employed spending
and tax hikes and inflation and wage and price

00:41:39.910 --> 00:41:41.869
controls, of course under a Republican.

00:41:41.869 --> 00:41:42.869
Had to be.

00:41:42.869 --> 00:41:44.010
Just great, Richard Nixon.

00:41:44.010 --> 00:41:45.930
I'm sure he was a fine man.

00:41:45.930 --> 00:41:52.280
I met him a few times, but he was a economic
moron and there's nothing we can do about

00:41:52.280 --> 00:41:53.440
that.

00:41:53.440 --> 00:41:55.780
It's history.

00:41:55.780 --> 00:41:59.460
Actually, I'll throw in LBJ.

00:41:59.460 --> 00:42:06.200
LBJ, Nixon, Ford, Carter, producing every
Keynesian trick in the bag, and this country

00:42:06.200 --> 00:42:14.090
suffered mightily with inflation and unemployment
rising together.

00:42:14.090 --> 00:42:16.280
By the way, it damaged us enormously overseas.

00:42:16.280 --> 00:42:24.490
In the current situation ... Look, I believe
Republicans and Democrats frankly are pretty

00:42:24.490 --> 00:42:26.960
equally to blame.

00:42:26.960 --> 00:42:30.570
Since 2000 or 2001 we've grown by less than
2% a year.

00:42:30.570 --> 00:42:33.560
We have had Republican president, Democrat
president.

00:42:33.560 --> 00:42:35.560
We've had Republican congress, Democrat Congress.

00:42:35.560 --> 00:42:37.150
We've grown by less than 2% a year.

00:42:37.150 --> 00:42:39.349
You can look it up.

00:42:39.349 --> 00:42:44.349
We've tried the same funny business, targeted
tax credits and increases of spending and

00:42:44.349 --> 00:42:49.079
subsidies and destroying the value of the
dollar, overregulation.

00:42:49.079 --> 00:42:54.950
We're not at here at dinner.

00:42:54.950 --> 00:42:57.640
You can't get a loan if you're in the gun
business.

00:42:57.640 --> 00:43:00.070
You actually can't hardly get a loan.

00:43:00.070 --> 00:43:01.329
Maybe it's just beginning to start.

00:43:01.329 --> 00:43:03.420
C&amp;I loans are starting to rise.

00:43:03.420 --> 00:43:10.210
Dodd-Frank has completely transmogrified and
stultified the entire banking business to

00:43:10.210 --> 00:43:12.480
the point where bankers don't want to take
risk anymore.

00:43:12.480 --> 00:43:20.460
Bankers are in the business of taking risks,
bit if you lose on the risk you lose money.

00:43:20.460 --> 00:43:24.030
If you lose money, you know what?

00:43:24.030 --> 00:43:30.640
One of our cherished legal traditions: if
you lose money on a consistent basis you go

00:43:30.640 --> 00:43:32.140
bankrupt.

00:43:32.140 --> 00:43:35.160
Hello, bankrupt.

00:43:35.160 --> 00:43:37.150
We have laws and judges.

00:43:37.150 --> 00:43:40.839
Whatever happened to that?

00:43:40.839 --> 00:43:47.790
You have regulators standing over the shoulders
of every bankers so they won't make a so-called

00:43:47.790 --> 00:43:48.790
bad loan.

00:43:48.790 --> 00:43:52.920
The EPA is trying to stop the very revolution
of fossil fuels that has given us now energy

00:43:52.920 --> 00:43:55.930
independence, world dominance, and better
economic growth.

00:43:55.930 --> 00:43:57.670
I don't need to go on and on and on.

00:43:57.670 --> 00:44:00.400
I'm just saying you are right, sir.

00:44:00.400 --> 00:44:08.010
The trouble with that is it just never seems
to end.

00:44:08.010 --> 00:44:10.930
We've got some pretty good people on our side.

00:44:10.930 --> 00:44:18.599
The chairman of the economics department of
Harvard University, Greg Mankiw, he's a good

00:44:18.599 --> 00:44:19.599
guy.

00:44:19.599 --> 00:44:24.910
He's a terrific guy, served under W as the
head of the Council of Economic Advisors.

00:44:24.910 --> 00:44:29.440
He's said it's become obvious now the difference
between right and wrong.

00:44:29.440 --> 00:44:34.230
The dean of the Columbia business school,
Len Hubbard, who was also a former CEA chairman

00:44:34.230 --> 00:44:42.410
under W. Larry Summers, who was really kind
of a moderate Clinton-Rubin conservative guy

00:44:42.410 --> 00:44:50.930
in the 90s, comes back under Obama and sounds
like John Kenneth Galbraith or something like

00:44:50.930 --> 00:44:51.930
that.

00:44:51.930 --> 00:44:53.180
Who was Ken Galbraith?

00:44:53.180 --> 00:44:55.170
Nevermind, it's not even important.

00:44:55.170 --> 00:44:59.250
He was a big library who spend more money.

00:44:59.250 --> 00:45:01.400
I don't know what to tell you.

00:45:01.400 --> 00:45:02.530
The battle goes on.

00:45:02.530 --> 00:45:06.650
Look, at the end of the day it's really easy
in a way.

00:45:06.650 --> 00:45:11.120
At the end of the day it's a political questions,
and at the end of the day you come back to

00:45:11.120 --> 00:45:12.120
what I said to you before.

00:45:12.120 --> 00:45:13.599
You're going to have to fight like hell.

00:45:13.599 --> 00:45:15.589
This is a democracy.

00:45:15.589 --> 00:45:18.050
America is a great democracy.

00:45:18.050 --> 00:45:20.680
It's one of the reasons why I'm so optimistic.

00:45:20.680 --> 00:45:23.981
When America sees things going really badly
it makes changes.

00:45:23.981 --> 00:45:27.420
It makes management changes through the ballot
box.

00:45:27.420 --> 00:45:29.020
That's the way this thing's going to be settled.

00:45:29.020 --> 00:45:30.020
That's the only way.

00:45:30.020 --> 00:45:32.440
But I will say this to you, sir.

00:45:32.440 --> 00:45:37.119
It's pretty clear now that we know what to
do.

00:45:37.119 --> 00:45:43.570
You and I and a few others in this room could
sit down and fix the economy in 25 minutes.

00:45:43.570 --> 00:45:45.530
Honestly, we know what to do.

00:45:45.530 --> 00:45:47.290
The foreign policy thing?

00:45:47.290 --> 00:45:51.230
Much more difficult, much more difficult.

00:45:51.230 --> 00:45:58.990
But we know the difference between right and
wrong.

00:45:58.990 --> 00:46:01.500
How we lost in 2012 I will never understand.

00:46:01.500 --> 00:46:06.200
That was worse than Dewey and Truman, honest
to God.

00:46:06.200 --> 00:46:13.910
You have to stand up and tell the voters what
you believe in.

00:46:13.910 --> 00:46:18.369
That's the essence of politics.

00:46:18.369 --> 00:46:22.440
I'm reading in the newspapers how Jeb Bush
has got all this money locked up in the nomination.

00:46:22.440 --> 00:46:23.440
By the way, I like Jeb Bush.

00:46:23.440 --> 00:46:27.950
This is not about Jeb Bush, but I'm just saying
that idea which we saw four years ago with

00:46:27.950 --> 00:46:33.450
Mitt Romney, who's a fine man, terrible politician,
had all this money locked up.

00:46:33.450 --> 00:46:39.800
I hear it on the radio where some kind of
anniversary of The Beatles ... Remember The

00:46:39.800 --> 00:46:41.260
Beatles?

00:46:41.260 --> 00:46:44.380
Money can't buy you love.

00:46:44.380 --> 00:46:47.320
Think about that as a political axiom.

00:46:47.320 --> 00:46:48.390
Money can't buy you love.

00:46:48.390 --> 00:46:51.460
You know what buys you love?

00:46:51.460 --> 00:46:59.970
Message, believes, and the character and strength
like a Churchill to stand up and fight for

00:46:59.970 --> 00:47:06.960
it regardless of whatever short-term political
problems may exist.

00:47:06.960 --> 00:47:12.070
We have a group called The Committee to Unleash
American Prosperity started by Steve Forbes,

00:47:12.070 --> 00:47:14.079
myself, Steve Moore, and Art Laffer.

00:47:14.079 --> 00:47:18.920
We're forming a committee of very distinguished
business men and women and academics, and

00:47:18.920 --> 00:47:22.040
we are asking these candidates to come to
meetings.

00:47:22.040 --> 00:47:26.460
We started the first couple in and your, going
to go around the country to have more where

00:47:26.460 --> 00:47:28.599
they will be directly ... It's not a debate.

00:47:28.599 --> 00:47:34.210
They will sit down, have a nice little supper,
and we'll directly answer questions from knowledgeable,

00:47:34.210 --> 00:47:35.960
distinguished people.

00:47:35.960 --> 00:47:36.960
That's what we're going to do.

00:47:36.960 --> 00:47:40.329
I want that done.

00:47:40.329 --> 00:47:42.050
I have so much respect for these guys.

00:47:42.050 --> 00:47:45.270
I've known them for years, all of them.

00:47:45.270 --> 00:47:49.280
I want to hear them actually what I want to
hear them say.

00:47:49.280 --> 00:47:52.240
I want to hear them actually what I want to
hear them say.

00:47:52.240 --> 00:47:54.360
I don't want to fence around it.

00:47:54.360 --> 00:47:56.520
I don't want to hedge around it.

00:47:56.520 --> 00:48:00.410
I don't want to say one thing on the debate
and the next night have my press guy said

00:48:00.410 --> 00:48:01.410
I didn't believe it.

00:48:01.410 --> 00:48:03.349
I don't want any of that crap.

00:48:03.349 --> 00:48:06.560
I want clear thinking and message.

00:48:06.560 --> 00:48:07.650
You know what?

00:48:07.650 --> 00:48:08.819
Here's a awful thing.

00:48:08.819 --> 00:48:09.819
Here's a awful thing.

00:48:09.819 --> 00:48:19.180
In terms of message in 2012, Obama's left-wing
message was a lot clearer.

00:48:19.180 --> 00:48:20.980
He was the optimist.

00:48:20.980 --> 00:48:24.700
Our guy, I interviewed him, I love him.

00:48:24.700 --> 00:48:25.990
He was so good to me.

00:48:25.990 --> 00:48:28.089
I probably interviewed him five or six times.

00:48:28.089 --> 00:48:32.750
I would sit across from him knee to knee and
I to this day couldn't tell you what he really

00:48:32.750 --> 00:48:34.319
believes in.

00:48:34.319 --> 00:48:38.099
I'm probably smarter than your average bear.

00:48:38.099 --> 00:48:40.150
I couldn't tell you.

00:48:40.150 --> 00:48:42.700
Obama, on the other hand, had his talking
points.

00:48:42.700 --> 00:48:45.710
You see his guys, campaign managers, on Charlie
Rose last night?

00:48:45.710 --> 00:48:47.540
God, eat your heart out.

00:48:47.540 --> 00:48:48.900
They're so clever.

00:48:48.900 --> 00:48:53.800
Why can't we do that, conservatives get here's
what we believe in.

00:48:53.800 --> 00:48:55.180
That's what's going to win.

00:48:55.180 --> 00:48:59.170
The academic studies, sir, you are right,
but I don't know.

00:48:59.170 --> 00:49:02.910
It hasn't happened in my lifetime and probably
never will.

00:49:02.910 --> 00:49:03.910
Other questions.

00:49:03.910 --> 00:49:04.910
I love this.

00:49:04.910 --> 00:49:06.250
Come on, fire away.

00:49:06.250 --> 00:49:07.570
Disagree, yell at me.

00:49:07.570 --> 00:49:08.570
It's okay.

00:49:08.570 --> 00:49:09.839
I do this for a living.

00:49:09.839 --> 00:49:10.839
Yes.

00:49:10.839 --> 00:49:24.579
Speaker 5: The American populous seems to
want ... Can you hear me up there, Mr. Kudlow?

00:49:24.579 --> 00:49:30.940
Larry Kudlow: I just have to wet my whistle.

00:49:30.940 --> 00:49:41.589
Speaker 5: American populous seems to want
congress, the left and the right, to legislate

00:49:41.589 --> 00:49:43.440
from the middle.

00:49:43.440 --> 00:49:47.290
In your opinion, are our countries solvable
from the middle.

00:49:47.290 --> 00:49:51.420
Larry Kudlow: All depends where the middle
is, I guess.

00:49:51.420 --> 00:49:57.530
This is a right of center country, has been
for a long time.

00:49:57.530 --> 00:50:01.400
My former boss Ronald Reagan used to say the
following.

00:50:01.400 --> 00:50:04.150
He said two things.

00:50:04.150 --> 00:50:16.829
One, if you and I agree on 80% of an issue
and we disagree on 20%, we are not enemies.

00:50:16.829 --> 00:50:23.500
Number two, Reagan would say give me half
a loaf now and I'll get the other half later.

00:50:23.500 --> 00:50:24.500
I was in the room.

00:50:24.500 --> 00:50:25.500
I was a kid.

00:50:25.500 --> 00:50:26.910
I didn't sit at the cabinet table.

00:50:26.910 --> 00:50:28.900
I sat at that chair against wall.

00:50:28.900 --> 00:50:31.240
I heard him.

00:50:31.240 --> 00:50:34.240
Now, Reagan made a lot of deals.

00:50:34.240 --> 00:50:35.770
Not a lot of them were good.

00:50:35.770 --> 00:50:40.560
He knows that, said so in his memoirs, particularly
the '82 spending deal.

00:50:40.560 --> 00:50:44.829
He never got spending cuts for some minor
tax cuts, but blah blah blah. '81 he made

00:50:44.829 --> 00:50:47.250
a brilliant deal on taxes and spending.

00:50:47.250 --> 00:50:53.670
‘83 they salvaged Social Security for four
decades, '86 on tax reform.

00:50:53.670 --> 00:50:55.130
These were all deals.

00:50:55.130 --> 00:50:57.010
They were deals on the MX missile.

00:50:57.010 --> 00:50:59.540
There were deals on setting up the les in
Europe.

00:50:59.540 --> 00:51:01.670
I don't want to go through all the deals.

00:51:01.670 --> 00:51:06.140
Reagan was one of the greatest labor negotiators
in history.

00:51:06.140 --> 00:51:07.140
People forgot that.

00:51:07.140 --> 00:51:08.550
The guy ran the Screen Actors Guild.

00:51:08.550 --> 00:51:12.740
He was president of the Screen Actors Guild
seven times.

00:51:12.740 --> 00:51:19.359
To this day he led the only strike of the
Screen Actors Guild against in those days

00:51:19.359 --> 00:51:22.140
the movie bosses, the only strike ever.

00:51:22.140 --> 00:51:25.500
I'm still a member of the Screen Actors Guild
because I did a bunch of commercials years

00:51:25.500 --> 00:51:26.500
ago.

00:51:26.500 --> 00:51:28.141
I don't recommend it.

00:51:28.141 --> 00:51:33.990
It's not my kind of crowd but Reagan ran it
seven times, so he know how to face down Louie

00:51:33.990 --> 00:51:34.990
Mayer.

00:51:34.990 --> 00:51:41.190
In that sense Tip O'Neill was nothing.

00:51:41.190 --> 00:51:42.330
In answer to your question.

00:51:42.330 --> 00:51:43.650
I don't know where the middle is.

00:51:43.650 --> 00:51:48.500
I think the middle's a lot further to the
right than other people may.

00:51:48.500 --> 00:51:50.330
I'm not afraid to make a deal.

00:51:50.330 --> 00:51:56.290
As long as you don't give up your principles,
such a thing is possible.

00:51:56.290 --> 00:51:58.890
Presidents have done this before.

00:51:58.890 --> 00:52:05.450
This guy in the White House, he doesn't even
want to sit down with you.

00:52:05.450 --> 00:52:10.540
You go back to 2011 ... This is probably going
to be very unpopular but I'm going to stand

00:52:10.540 --> 00:52:15.060
up for my old friend John Boehner, who was
an original Kemp supply sider.

00:52:15.060 --> 00:52:17.300
Jack Kemp was my mentor.

00:52:17.300 --> 00:52:22.560
Boehner had a deal with Obama on deficits,
entitlements and taxes.

00:52:22.560 --> 00:52:24.690
Not tax rates but tax revenues from growth.

00:52:24.690 --> 00:52:29.420
He had a deal in 2011 and he walks out of
the White House.

00:52:29.420 --> 00:52:34.440
Then they hear the next hour or two that the
deal's been changed and Obama wanted another

00:52:34.440 --> 00:52:35.619
400 billion dollars in taxes.

00:52:35.619 --> 00:52:38.840
He knew he was breaking the deal.

00:52:38.840 --> 00:52:42.720
He did it purposely to avoid the deal.

00:52:42.720 --> 00:52:45.270
Since then we have not had a good sit down.

00:52:45.270 --> 00:52:48.200
That was the end of it.

00:52:48.200 --> 00:52:50.280
This is the tragedy of the story.

00:52:50.280 --> 00:52:54.790
Reagan would never let that happen.

00:52:54.790 --> 00:52:57.480
People say how Tip O'Neill this and that.

00:52:57.480 --> 00:52:59.680
Let me just tell you something.

00:52:59.680 --> 00:53:01.150
I knew Tip O'Neill.

00:53:01.150 --> 00:53:03.359
I knew his staff, his speechwriters.

00:53:03.359 --> 00:53:06.609
Tip O'Neill was no Ronald Reagan.

00:53:06.609 --> 00:53:08.850
Reagan rolled O'Neill continuously.

00:53:08.850 --> 00:53:10.010
You know why?

00:53:10.010 --> 00:53:11.660
We had the votes.

00:53:11.660 --> 00:53:17.040
We had a majority in the senate for six years
and we didn't have the house, but we did have

00:53:17.040 --> 00:53:23.550
the house because then conservative boll weevil
Democrats voted Reagan so we could command

00:53:23.550 --> 00:53:31.010
260-70 votes on every roll call, kicked Tip
O'Neill's assess, and get a decent deal done.

00:53:31.010 --> 00:53:38.420
I'm yearning for those days and I think somehow
one of these governors ... I really do want

00:53:38.420 --> 00:53:39.420
a governor.

00:53:39.420 --> 00:53:42.060
I think the first term senate model has been
tried and found lacking.

00:53:42.060 --> 00:53:45.270
I want a governor.

00:53:45.270 --> 00:53:49.950
I believe from one of them will come such
a person.

00:53:49.950 --> 00:53:51.890
Why do I believe that?

00:53:51.890 --> 00:53:57.589
A, because I believe in America and its democracy,
B, because I believe in God.

00:53:57.589 --> 00:53:59.319
I'm going to wind it up right there.

00:53:59.319 --> 00:54:00.090
Thank you very much.

